\section*{Is the exponent of matrix multiplication 2}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 107; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that characteristic-zero matrix multiplication has square exponent below $2.258$, dual exponent above $0.465$, and rectangular exponent at aspect $0.709$ below $2.092$, with the stated finite-characteristic exceptions; In manuscript 2 (family 107; 2026-10-02), the asserted result is that the complex matrix-multiplication exponent is at most $9/4$; In manuscript 3 (family 107; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that the square multiplication exponent is below $2.371054886006746$ over every fixed field, including every positive characteristic.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
In exact complex arithmetic, let $\omega$ be the square matrix-multiplication exponent, $w(k)$ the exponent for multiplying an $n\times n^k$ matrix by an $n^k\times n$ matrix, and $\alpha=\sup\{k:w(k)=2\}$. The claimed bounds are
\[
 \omega<1129/500=2.258,\qquad\alpha>0.465,\qquad w(0.709)<2.092.
\]
These are arithmetic exponents, rather than bit-complexity or finite-precision bounds.

\paragraph{Manuscript 2.}
For every $\varepsilon>0$, the claim gives an exact complex-arithmetic algorithm multiplying two $n\times n$ matrices in
\[
 O_{\varepsilon}(n^{9/4+\varepsilon})\quad\text{operations},\qquad\omega\le9/4.
\]
Its associated tensor inequality uses $T=\sum_{i=1}^sT_i$ with every $T_i\ne0$, a shared first tensor factor and matched disjoint sectors on the other two. A tensor character $\lambda$ takes nonnegative real values and is additive, multiplicative and restriction-monotone, with $\lambda(1)=1$; define $p_X$ by $\lambda(x\sum_{j=1}^my_jz_j)=m^{p_X}$. For every probability vector $q$ the claimed inequality is
\[
 \lambda(T)\ge e^{p_XH(q)}\prod_i\lambda(T_i)^{q_i},\qquad
 H(q)=-\sum_iq_i\log q_i,
\]
with $0\log0=0$. These are exact arithmetic and tensor-character statements, without finite-precision assumptions.

\paragraph{Manuscript 3.}
For every fixed field $F$, the claimed arithmetic exponent of square matrix multiplication satisfies
\[
 \omega_F<2.371054886006746<2.371054887.
\]
The exponent is the infimum of asymptotic powers achievable by exact field operations, allowing an arbitrarily small additive exponent slack. Positive and zero characteristics are both included.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 107; 2026-09-24), an auxiliary tensor supplies a conditional label to the missing third leg while retaining a dot-product block. Recursive completion trees and finite-family entropy optimization produce certified parameters; a strict comparison argument verifies the advertised boundaries.

For manuscript 2 (family 107; 2026-10-02), tensor degenerations separate blocks that already expose two independent variable legs. Character inequalities for polynomial multiplication, a determinant filtration, and discrete growth bound the detecting character values for matrix multiplication at the claimed exponent.

For manuscript 3 (family 107; 2026-09-24), a joint extraction from several tensor depths uses entropy and a common modular hash. Sequential deletion assigns variables compatibly, and exact integer tables repair inherited masks; the resulting direct sum is converted to an exponent bound by the rank comparison.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The stated arithmetic exponent bounds, including $9/4$ over the complex numbers, remain strictly above two. The requested exponent-two multiplication over the rational field is therefore not established, and arithmetic-operation estimates alone do not assert a bit-time implementation.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 107; 2026-09-24), the documented Lean scope is the stated matrix-multiplication exponent estimates over $\mathbb C$, including the rectangular bounds; the declarations do not extend these estimates to all characteristic-zero fields or the claimed finite-characteristic range.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

For manuscript 3 (family 107; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: the square multiplication exponent is below $2.371054886006746$ over every fixed field, including every positive characteristic.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

No Lean formalization is listed at this snapshot for manuscript 2 (family 107; 2026-10-02).

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{MatrixFields.lean}, \texttt{MatrixMultiplication.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.MatrixAllFields.MatrixMultiplication.AllFieldMain.omega\_lt\_source\_constant}, \texttt{OAI.MatrixMultiplication.complex\_alpha\_gt\_93\_div\_200}, \texttt{OAI.MatrixMultiplication.complex\_omega\_le\_nine\_quarters}, \texttt{OAI.MatrixMultiplication.complex\_rectangular\_omega\_lt\_523\_div\_250}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

